          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What screening tool does guidance describe for anxiety, and what does the score change?",
          "answer": "The guidance I could retrieve names the **seven-item generalized anxiety disorder questionnaire (GAD-7)** as the instrument most often used to screen for anxiety and to track its severity. Source: *Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care* (preventive services and psychiatric association guidance, educational summary).\n\n**What the score changes, per that document:**\n- **Severity bands:** 5 is mild, 10 is moderate, and 15 is severe.\n- **Threshold for further assessment:** a score of 10 is the point at which further assessment usually follows.\n\nA score is a prompt for a clinician conversation, not a diagnosis on its own. Only a clinician can assess what a score means for a particular person.\n\n**Who screening is for:** The same document says screening is described for adults where systems are in place to ensure accurate diagnosis and follow-up. The strongest statement is for adults under 65. For older adults, the document says the evidence is insufficient to make a recommendation either way. That describes the strength of the evidence. It is not a recommendation against screening.\n\n**What I could not confirm:**\n- **Management steps the score informs:** my search did not return a passage on this, so I can't say what treatment or follow-up steps guidance links to each band.\n- **Where authorities disagree:** the anxiety document I found has no disagreement section. My broader search failed before it could check for differing positions, so I can't say whether authorities disagree on these bands or thresholds.\n\nIf you want those two points, a clinician or a dedicated guideline search would be the next step.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
